-- title:
--   Zero divisor of x-a has degree [F:K(x)]
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra satisfying `IsCurveOver K F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ admits a finitely supported divisor $P$ with $P(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v f$ at every place and $\deg P = 0$; each residue field $v.\mathrm{ResidueField}$ is finite over $K$; and $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$. Here a place $v$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\operatorname{im}(K \to F)$, distinct from $F$ itself and a principal ideal ring, with $\operatorname{ord}_v f = -\log v(f)$ for the adic valuation of the associated height-one prime. Assume also that $\mathrm{LSpace}\,0 = \{f : v(f) \le 1 \text{ for all } v\}$ is finite-dimensional over $K$. Let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $K$, with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$, let $a \in K$, and let $D$ be a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on places with $D(v) = \max(0, \operatorname{ord}_v(x - a))$ for all $v$, i.e. the divisor of zeros of $x - a$. Then $\deg D = \sum_v D(v)\cdot \deg v$ equals $[F : K(x)]$, viewed in $\mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   This is the standard statement that the fibre of the map $x : X \to \mathbb{P}^1$ over a rational point $a \in K$, counted with multiplicities and residue degrees, has size the degree $[F : K(x)]$; it is the companion, over a finite point, of the corresponding statement for the pole divisor of $x$. It is used in counting places with prescribed behaviour over an algebraically closed base field, where every place has degree $1$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Divisor_degree_eq_finrank_adjoin_of_eq_max_ord_sub_algebraMap.lean

